          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love you, Your Majesty.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.98,\"reason\":\"Cordelia directly presents her love for Lear as a true emotional state. The surrounding qualification limits its degree rather than changing the utterance from an avowal.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.86,\"reason\":\"The expression invokes Cordelia's existing filial bond rather than making a new promise. Cordelia represents herself as continuing to owe Lear a daughter's love and later identifies associated duties of obedience and honour. Lear does not accept this as an adequate response to his requested public declaration.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"According to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Return those duties back as are right fit,\\nObey you, love you, and most honour you.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Mend your speech a little,\\nLest you may mar your fortunes.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The love expression is a controlled, explicitly measured avowal, not a reflex-like affective discharge. Cordelia says she cannot pour her heart directly into speech and carefully restricts her claim to what her bond warrants.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"no more nor less.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.84,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing love and invoking filial obligation, the expression functions as a deliberately delimited answer—and implicit refusal to participate in Lear's demand for competitive, expansive praise. Its restraint directly contrasts with the requested declaration of loving him most.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"According to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Cordelia speaks publicly to her father and king after he asks his daughters to compete in declaring who loves him most, with inheritance tied to their answers. After initially answering “Nothing,” she avows only the love due under her filial bond and refuses to exaggerate it.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Cordelia invokes an existing filial obligation: she presents herself as owing and continuing to give Lear the love, obedience, and honour properly due from a daughter. She does not clearly undertake a new commitment in the focal words. Lear rejects the formulation as inadequate and urges her to revise it, so it receives no affirmative uptake within the supplied passage.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied title, characters, and scene identify this as the opening love-test scene of Shakespeare's King Lear. The assessment uses only the supplied text.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage strongly supports the represented avowal and supplies internal evidence of Cordelia's affection, but it cannot independently establish her inner sincerity as a fact beyond the literary representation. The exact scope of the filial “bond” is stated only generally, and the supplied excerpt ends before Lear's final response or any later consequences.\"}"
        }
      ],
